> > I measure autumn densities of grouse prior to commencement of hunting. =
The=20
> > main goal of the censuses is to monitor whether hunting affects populat=
ion=20

> > development thus precise estimates of change in different areas are=20
> > needed. The design is with permanent lines visiting the same set of=20

> > transects each year to get an estimate of trend with high precision. I =
use=20

> > pointing dogs to find grouse while walking the line transects. When the=

=20

> > dog finds a grouse it freezes and it is possible for the dog handler to=

=20
> > walk to the dog and then give commando to flush the birds. Test on radi=
o-
> > collared grouse shows no responsive movements. The lines are walked by =
at=20

> > least two persons; one handles the dog and one keep track of the line=20

> > transect. When the birds are flushed the perpendicular distances from t=
he=20

> > birds original place to the line transect are measured.  The person=20
> > keeping track of the line has often more than two dogs in leach so the=20

> > dogs that are on search can be altered. This means that on the line the=
re=20

> > are always at least one man and two or more dogs and the dog handler.=20

> > Crisscrossing ahead of this equipage is one dog at the time on search f=
or=20

> > grouse. It is not unusual that the equipage on the line flushes grouse=20

> > independent of the dog on search. The same situation with man flushing=20

> > grouse can happened when the dog handler leaves the line transect and=20

> > approaches the dog on stand (although this is not as common as flushing=
 is=20
> > directly on the line). Dog-flushing and man-flushing=92s are distinguis=
hed=20

> > and registered with perpendicular distance on the field protocol.=20

> > Is it possible to treat the equipage on the line and the dog on search =
as=20
> > two teams of observers? The equipage on the line searches on and close =
to=20

> > the line and the second team (dog) searches ahead of the equipage. The=20

> > equipage on the line acts independently of the dog and will always see =
the=20
> > same animal as the dog (duplicate sightings). Duplicates only occur if =
the=20
> > dog sees the bird first. The birds flushed by the equipage on the line =
are=20
> > missed observations for the dog. P(0) for the equipage on the line will=
 be=20
> > 1 and the pooled p(0) is 1. Can I treat this as a Buckland-Turnock mode=
,=20

> > where the secondary observer is aware of the sightings of the primary=20
> > observer in a trial configuration because of only one-way independence?=

 =20
> > My intention is to estimate p(0) for the dog and use this in a second=20
> > analysis where I delete all observations not made by the dog and then=20
> > adjust for g(0)<1 in a traditional distance sampling analysis. Am I=20
> > thinking right here?
